The timer lets a turn run on whose thread was bound to another core's units during it,
so a thread alone with spare time stays on the core it left for as long as the timer is deferred.

Caught-by: fuzz-smp2
Report: the timer lets the tick pass that moves the turn's thread to its core

diff --git a/kernel/sched.c b/kernel/sched.c
--- a/kernel/sched.c
+++ b/kernel/sched.c
@@ -831,7 +831,7 @@ uint32_t sched_wake_ticks(void)
         return 1;
     }
     /* A thread bound to another core's units during its turn here goes there as the next tick ends the turn. */
-    if (core->run_queue != 0 || (turn->time.type == CAP_BOUND && unit_core(turn->time.a) != core_index())) {
+    if (core->run_queue != 0) {
         return 1;
     }
     uint32_t balance = account_now(turn);
